srow(31, 6, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND ISRAEL HEARD" (GENESIS 35:22) — JACOB SHAKEN AT REUBEN, TOLD BY THE HOLY SPIRIT THAT REUBEN REPENTED ("THE SONS OF JACOB WERE TWELVE"); REUBEN FASTED ALL HIS DAYS UNTIL MOSES ACCEPTED HIM ("LET REUBEN LIVE", 33:6); JACOB AT HIS DEATH REBUKED HIS SONS ONE BY ONE (GENESIS 49) AND THEN ASKED THEM ALL: PERHAPS YOUR HEART IS DIVIDED FROM HIM WHO SPOKE AND THE WORLD WAS? THEY ANSWERED: "HEAR, O ISRAEL" OUR FATHER — AS YOUR HEART IS NOT DIVIDED, SO OURS IS NOT: "THE LORD OUR GOD, THE LORD IS ONE"; AND JACOB BOWED AT THE BED\'S HEAD (GENESIS 47:31) — HE GAVE THANKS THAT NO WORTHLESS SEED WAS AMONG THEM; SOME SAY REUBEN SAID "BLESSED BE THE NAME OF HIS GLORIOUS KINGDOM FOR EVER AND EVER"; THE HOLY ONE SAID TO JACOB: ALL YOUR DAYS YOU DESIRED THAT YOUR SONS GREET MORNING AND EVENING WITH THE SHEMA. THE PISKA\'S CROWN — THE CREED\'S FIRST UTTERANCE PUT IN THE SONS\' MOUTHS AT JACOB\'S DEATHBED, "ISRAEL" THE FATHER ADDRESSED ({SP_(31, 6, "נזדעזע")} "he was shaken"): the Shema read as a DIALOGUE — the sons\' answer to the father\'s doubt, the response line ("blessed be the name …") supplied between 6:4 and 6:5 for the recitation (the English\'s note: not scriptural, interjected in the liturgy) — the Talmud\'s seat Pesachim 56a (the docket\'s); "morning and evening" the times of 34:8. The ink: "hear" at 6:4 an IMPERATIVE SINGULAR (the morphology: the chapter\'s two imperatives, 6:4 "hear" and 6:12 "take heed" — computed). The middah: a law\'s words given a narrative seat (E28\'s kin — the verse read from the story before it; not a mashal — a history). Fresh in both files.')
